What are Synthetic Pyrethroids?
Synthetic Pyrethroids are man-made chemical analogues of the natural pyrethrins found in chrysanthemum flowers. They are synthesized to enhance the insecticidal properties of their natural counterparts, providing a broad spectrum of activity against external parasites such as flies, ticks, mites, fleas, lice, and mosquitoes. The insecticidal activity is derived from ketoalcoholic esters of chrysanthemic and pyrethroic acids, resulting in compounds that are highly effective while generally exhibiting lower mammalian toxicity compared to many older classes of insecticides.
As a cornerstone of modern pest management, these compounds are extensively used in formulations for agricultural sprays, household insecticides, and public health programs to combat disease vectors.
